  <h2>The three tables</h2>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>GDT + TSS — the segments nobody uses anymore, except for the stack</h3>
    <p>
      In 64-bit mode segmentation is mostly vestigial, but the CPU still needs valid code/data descriptors and,
      critically, the TSS for <em>stacks</em>. SIGRUN's GDT has 7 entries: null, kernel code/data
      (<code class="mono">0x08</code>/<code class="mono">0x10</code>), user data/code
      (<code class="mono">0x18</code>/<code class="mono">0x20</code> — order chosen to match the STAR MSR
      layout used by SYSRET), and a two-part TSS descriptor at <code class="mono">0x28</code>. The TSS carries
      <code class="mono">RSP0</code> (top of the IST stack — where the CPU switches to when ring-3 code takes
      an interrupt) and <code class="mono">IST1</code> (a dedicated double-fault stack, so a corrupted stack
      can't prevent the panic handler from running).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>IDT — 256 entries, installed once</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// idt.rs — a gate = handler offset (split across 3 fields) + selector + type</span>
pub fn set_handler(&amp;mut self, vector: u8,
                   handler: extern "x86-interrupt" fn(InterruptFrame)) &#123;
    self.entries[vector as usize] = IdtEntry::new(handler as u64, 0x08, 0x8E);
&#125;
<span class="c">// KERNEL_IDT is a 'static mut — lidt promises the table lives forever,</span>
<span class="c">// so it cannot be a stack-local.</span>
static mut KERNEL_IDT: Idt = Idt::new();</code></pre>
    <p>
      Handlers for exceptions 0–31 log what happened (page fault also reads CR2 — the faulting address) and
      halt. The LAPIC timer lands on vector 32 and spurious on 255. Note the handler declaration:
      <code class="mono">extern "x86-interrupt"</code> is the Rust ABI for interrupt stubs — the compiler emits
      the right prologue/epilogue (including <code class="mono">iretq</code>) and can even push the error code
      for exceptions that have one (<code class="mono">set_handler_with_error</code>).
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>APIC — the modern interrupt controller</h3>
    <p>
      The Local APIC is MMIO at <code class="mono">0xFEE00000</code> (identity-mapped by the boot
      <code class="mono">1&nbsp;GiB</code> page that covers 3–4&nbsp;GiB). SIGRUN enables it via the spurious
      register (also installing vector 255), zeros the task priority, and programs the timer. The legacy dual
      PIC (ports 0x20/0xA0) is remapped to vectors 0x20/0x28 and then <em>fully masked</em> — it stays dead so
      only the APIC delivers interrupts. EOI is a write of 0 to the <code class="mono">0xB0</code> register.
    </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// interrupt/mod.rs — the whole timer ISR, EOI FIRST</span>
pub extern "x86-interrupt" fn timer(_frame: InterruptFrame) &#123;
    unsafe &#123; LOCAL_APIC.eoi() &#125;;   <span class="c">// allow next interrupt even if we switch context</span>
    crate::timer::on_tick();       <span class="c">// advance clock → fire wheel → scheduler::tick()</span>
&#125;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Honest gaps here</span>
    <code class="mono">register_handler()</code>, <code class="mono">enable_irq()</code> and
    <code class="mono">disable_irq()</code> are currently no-ops — the timer is hardwired at vector 32 and no
    other device IRQ has a runtime registration path yet. The exception handlers also halt outright rather
    than killing just the faulting task (no exception-to-task mapping yet).
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: interrupts</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>What is <code class="mono">extern "x86-interrupt"</code> and why is it essential here?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          It's Rust's built-in ABI for interrupt handlers. The compiler knows the CPU has already pushed an
          interrupt frame, so the handler receives <code class="mono">&amp;InterruptFrame</code> as an argument
          instead of pretending normal calling-convention registers are intact, and it emits
          <code class="mono">iretq</code> (not a normal return) on exit. Without it you'd write a naked
          assembly stub per vector to save everything and fix the stack — the standard trick in C kernels.
          Because these are <code class="mono">#[feature(abi_x86_interrupt)]</code> functions, the flag lives
          with the crate root and, importantly, not in <code class="mono">lib.rs</code>, which must avoid
          double-compiling the <code class="mono">global_asm!</code> boot entry.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why must KERNEL_IDT be a static, and why IST1 for double fault?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          <code class="mono">lidt</code> expects the table to remain valid for the whole kernel lifetime; a
          static guarantees that, where a stack local would be a dangling pointer from the CPU's point of view.
          Preserving the IDT across the whole run is also why <code class="mono">load(&amp;'static self)</code>
          demands a <code class="mono">'static</code> reference in the signature. IST1 exists because a double
          fault means something went wrong <em>while handling an interrupt</em> — often a corrupted or
          exhausted stack. Switching to a fresh known-good stack (via the TSS interrupt-stack-table) lets the
          handler log and halt instead of triple-faulting into a reset.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "What would a triple fault do?" — On bare metal the CPU resets (often an infinite reboot loop); under QEMU it can just exit. It's the catastrophic tail of a double fault you failed to stack-switch out of.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>Why bother disabling the PIC if the APIC is there?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Because both controllers sit on the same delivery path, and the PIC's default vectors (0x08–0x0F)
          collide with CPU exceptions. SIGRUN remaps the PIC to 0x20/0x28 (a required ICW dance) then masks
          every line, so the APIC is the only source — this is the modern x86 pattern. Keeping the remap code
          is also useful: portable kernels still run on machines where the PIC must be initialized before the
          APIC can be trusted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why does the spurious handler send no EOI?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          A spurious interrupt is the APIC telling you "I had to deliver something, but the real interrupt
          disappeared" — typically when a device was masking/unmasking lines mid-delivery. If you EOI it, you
          can clear the LAPIC's in-service bit for an interrupt that <em>is</em> still pending, losing a real
          event. The firmware-accurate response — used by Linux too — is to return without EOI. SIGRUN's
          handler logs nothing and returns, which is exactly right.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

